action of proprietary capabilities from frontier models to train smaller, efficient counterparts[13][11][12].\n\n### Edge Deployment\nEdge AI has transitioned from a niche experiment to a standard deployment model for 2026[3][14]. The integration of SLMs into edge devices (smartphones, industrial sensors, robotics, and PCs) is driven by three \"hard truths\" of cloud-based AI:\n\n1.  **Latency:** Edge inference enables real-time responses (tens of milliseconds) by eliminating round-trips to distant data centers[3][15].\n2.  **Privacy/Security:** Sensitive data can be processed locally, ensuring compliance with regulations like the EU AI Act and keeping proprietary data within the enterprise perimeter[3][7][16].\n3.  **Reliability:** Edge systems remain functional offline, removing dependency on constant internet connectivity[3][16].\n\n### Enabling Technologies\n*   **Quantization & Pruning:** Techniques such as INT4/INT8 quantization and structural pruning have made it possible to shrink models to fit onto hardware with limited memory (RAM/VRAM) without significant loss in intelligence[3][16].\n*   **Hardware Acceleration:** Modern edge hardware, including dedicated NPUs (Neural Processing Units) and integrated AI acceleration in mainstream processors (e.g., Intel's Panther Lake), has reached a maturity level where running SLMs locally is increasingly efficient and cost-effective[17][18][16].\n\nBy 2026, the prevailing strategy for many enterprises is to use a **routing layer**—a system that directs simple, routine tasks to small, fast, local models, while reserving expensive frontier models only for complex, high-judgment workflows[2][19][6].\n\nSources:\n[1] [practicallogix.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHIFMTpmFpaTVOEK_FKAr7IkmFvWBcEZahzTFJ6mDN7VfGoAS2cwIWadj-7wZGR8zru1UjfCVd982-Y9z0SrwBwzcrRn7RSZOmnG5_A9XTJPMtcHiTRaEwxE-AEvfH43FqWh3jr3GEbg3ktvhtY5mHlH_Msqldozm-tpo3CdSDY1HVIhcKZ4izu4XdxkE85Ts219mGEMl9SmZaWgOrF1jHTtwUEni